From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 16:50:47 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(control): sendInput sends the bytes it was given (GD-15)

GhosttyControlCore::SendInput called ghostty_surface_text, which is
libghostty's paste entry point - its own doc comment says it is "treated
like a paste so this isn't useful for sending escape sequences". It was
not. input/paste.zig replaces every ESC, DEL, NUL, BS and control
character with a space and frames the result in ESC[200~/ESC[201~ when
mode 2004 is on, which is right for a paste and wrong for everything
else that reached it: the sendInput action, broadcast input
(RawWriteString), and any character falling through to SendCharEvent.

So a sendInput action could send literal text and nothing else, which is
almost never what one is for.

SendInput and SendCharEvent now use WriteToConnection - the literal byte
path libghostty's external termio backend already calls to reach WT's
conpty. PasteText keeps ghostty_surface_text and is now its only caller,
which is what that entry point was always for.

The open question the issue recorded was what ghostty_surface_text does
for input that a direct write would skip. Answered from the source: past
the paste encoder itself, completeClipboardPaste does one thing, a
scroll-to-bottom, and skipping it is correct here rather than a
shortcut. Cascadia does not snap the viewport for SendInput either -
TrySnapOnInput sits on the key and paste paths only - so this now
matches it on both paths.

Measured in a real ghostty pane against harness/rawin, which prints the
bytes the child receives, with mode 2004 on:

  sendInput <ESC>[31mREDTEXT<ESC>[0m -> <ESC>[31mREDTEXT<ESC>[0m
  sendInput AA<0x03>BB               -> AA<0x03>BB
  ctrl+v (unbound, so a char event)  -> <0x16>
  typing xy                          -> x, y
  paste HELLO                        -> <ESC>[200~HELLO<ESC>[201~

The first line is the bug: it used to arrive as
<ESC>[200~ [31mREDTEXT [0m<ESC>[201~. The last line is the regression
check - a real paste is still filtered and still framed, so GD-08 is
untouched.

Closes #15.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp    | 41 +++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
index 6b6f1f3a5..063a7c13d 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
@@ -813,6 +813,13 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         return ghostty_surface_key(_surface, ev);
     }
 
+    // A character the key path did not consume, written literally.
+    //
+    // Not ghostty_surface_text: that is libghostty's *paste* entry point, and
+    // input/paste.zig replaces every control character with a space. A char
+    // event is the user typing, so the bytes go through as they are - which is
+    // what cascadia does too, encoding the character and writing it to the
+    // connection rather than pasting it. See GD-15.
     bool GhosttyControlCore::SendCharEvent(const wchar_t ch, const WORD /*scanCode*/, const ControlKeyStates /*modifiers*/)
     {
         if (!_surface || _readOnly)
@@ -821,27 +828,46 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         }
 
         const wchar_t buf[2]{ ch, L'\0' };
-        const auto utf8 = til::u16u8(std::wstring_view{ buf, 1 });
-        ghostty_surface_text(_surface, utf8.data(), utf8.size());
+        WriteToConnection(til::u16u8(std::wstring_view{ buf, 1 }));
         return true;
     }
 
+    // Bytes the user deliberately asked to send: the `sendInput` action and
+    // broadcast input (RawWriteString). Literal - unfiltered and unframed.
+    //
+    // This used to call ghostty_surface_text, whose own doc comment says it is
+    // "treated like a paste so this isn't useful for sending escape
+    // sequences". It was not: every ESC in a sendInput action arrived as a
+    // space, wrapped in a ESC[200~/ESC[201~ pair the action never asked for
+    // (GD-15). Only PasteText wants that encoder, and only PasteText calls it.
+    //
+    // What a direct write skips is libghostty's scroll-to-bottom, which
+    // completeClipboardPaste does on the way through. That is the paste's
+    // behaviour, not this path's: cascadia does not snap the viewport for
+    // SendInput either - TrySnapOnInput sits on the key and paste paths only -
+    // so not scrolling here is the parity behaviour rather than an omission.
     void GhosttyControlCore::SendInput(std::wstring_view wstr)
     {
-        if (!_surface || _readOnly || wstr.empty())
+        if (_readOnly || wstr.empty())
         {
             return;
         }
-        const auto utf8 = til::u16u8(wstr);
-        ghostty_surface_text(_surface, utf8.data(), utf8.size());
+        WriteToConnection(til::u16u8(wstr));
     }
 
     void GhosttyControlCore::PasteText(const winrt::hstring& hstr)
     {
+        if (!_surface || _readOnly)
+        {
+            return;
+        }
+
         // Non-empty text needs nothing from us: ghostty_surface_text is
         // libghostty's paste entry point, so it already fenceposts the data
         // when mode 2004 is on and strips the bytes xterm strips. Adding our
-        // own ESC[200~ here would send two of them.
+        // own ESC[200~ here would send two of them. This is the one caller
+        // that wants that encoder - SendInput stopped routing through it in
+        // GD-15.
         //
         // Empty text is the exception, and it is not a no-op. An empty
         // bracketed paste is how an application learns a paste happened with
@@ -862,7 +888,8 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
             return;
         }
 
-        SendInput(hstr);
+        const auto utf8 = til::u16u8(std::wstring_view{ hstr });
+        ghostty_surface_text(_surface, utf8.data(), utf8.size());
     }
 
     void GhosttyControlCore::GotFocus()
